WEBVTT

00:06.473 --> 00:08.541
My name is Catherine Coleman Flowers.

00:08.541 --> 00:12.345
I am the founder of the Alabama Center
for Rule Enterprise,

00:12.645 --> 00:15.482
and our work is primarily in Lawrence
County, Alabama,

00:15.482 --> 00:18.485
between Selma and Montgomery,
which is where I'm from.

00:19.119 --> 00:23.690
When I moved back to Alabama in 2000,
I saw that in rural communities,

00:23.690 --> 00:27.694
especially my own community, people
were living with raw sewage on the ground,

00:28.128 --> 00:32.499
and I just didn't think that that
should be the case at this point in time.

00:33.967 --> 00:35.502
And a lot of these communities,

00:35.502 --> 00:38.338
there was no investment
and no infrastructure.

00:38.338 --> 00:42.942
So when people flush the toilet, the raw
sewage is going out on top of the ground.

00:43.276 --> 00:45.745
It's called street paving

00:45.745 --> 00:49.849
for people who have been arrested,
who could not afford site septic systems.

00:50.116 --> 00:51.951
And we found that those systems don't work
either.

00:51.951 --> 00:53.553
They still failed.

00:53.553 --> 00:56.556
But I'm talking about something
as basic as flushing the toilet

00:56.556 --> 00:59.559
and not being able
to see it in your front yard.

01:00.060 --> 01:03.863
No one has acknowledged
the lack of infrastructure investment

01:03.863 --> 01:06.866
in rural communities,
especially for rural communities

01:07.100 --> 01:09.602
in the Black
Belt region of the United States.

01:09.602 --> 01:13.039
And I'm finding out that this is true
of rural communities around the country.

01:14.040 --> 01:16.209
Those
are the issues that we should resolve,

01:16.209 --> 01:18.278
though,
in the first half of the last century,

01:18.278 --> 01:22.816
when we had the Rockefeller Sanitary
Commission trying to eliminate hookworm

01:23.049 --> 01:24.684
in the United States,

01:24.684 --> 01:29.222
but in these communities, they have always
been victims of hyper inequality.

01:29.456 --> 01:31.925
It still exists, and it's getting worse.

01:31.925 --> 01:34.861
It's in the intersection
of this type of injustice

01:34.861 --> 01:37.363
with sewage on the ground,
and it's getting warmer.

01:37.363 --> 01:40.667
The possibility of illnesses
that we've never seen before,

01:40.667 --> 01:43.103
a cropping or illnesses
they simply don't test for

01:43.103 --> 01:47.040
because they assume that this is a problem
in third world nations around the world.

01:47.340 --> 01:49.008
But we actually have those kinds

01:49.008 --> 01:52.345
of conditions undergirded by inequality
here in this country.

01:52.946 --> 01:55.949
And climate change
is going to make the situation worse.

01:56.516 --> 01:58.985
I got engaged and had to find partners

01:58.985 --> 02:02.155
and collaborators
that can help us solve this problem.

02:02.188 --> 02:06.526
First of all, stop the health department
from arresting people in Lawrence County.

02:06.793 --> 02:10.530
And then the second part of it,
which is defining technological solutions

02:10.530 --> 02:11.197
that will work.

02:12.665 --> 02:13.166
What we've

02:13.166 --> 02:16.169
done is launch a wastewater challenge.

02:16.569 --> 02:20.140
We have engaged universities, Baylor's

02:20.140 --> 02:23.143
National School of Tropical Medicine
as part of that collaborative.

02:23.443 --> 02:26.813
We're about to publish a study with Baylor
where we found

02:26.813 --> 02:29.816
evidence of tropical diseases
in lands County.

02:30.683 --> 02:34.954
And before we start promoting technologies
whole heartedly, we want to test.

02:34.954 --> 02:36.956
And that's our next phase.

02:36.956 --> 02:39.759
Duke University
is part of that collaboration.

02:39.759 --> 02:43.163
We're just hoping that foundations
that fund these kinds of efforts around

02:43.163 --> 02:45.465
the world
will start looking inward as well,

02:45.465 --> 02:48.468
and fund a wastewater
challenge here in this country.

02:49.502 --> 02:52.372
I think the first step is get engaged,
be involved.

02:52.372 --> 02:56.009
I think democracy doesn't work
when people are not informed.

02:56.209 --> 02:59.012
A lot of people don't know what's going on
in their communities if they know

02:59.012 --> 03:03.449
what's going on, how their representatives
are voting, who's funding them.

03:03.750 --> 03:06.352
I think that
that would make a big difference.

03:06.352 --> 03:07.921
I think there are a number of things
they can do.

03:07.921 --> 03:09.656
People don't have to do what I do.

03:09.656 --> 03:11.791
There's so many things that are
needed in the communities.

03:13.793 --> 03:15.061
My story may be different

03:15.061 --> 03:18.064
from others
because I grew up with activist parents

03:18.097 --> 03:22.202
in the movement for the right to vote
in the 1960s, and there were people

03:22.202 --> 03:25.171
who are now historical figures
that were regulars at our home.

03:25.305 --> 03:28.975
So I always felt that
if change had to occur, that

03:28.975 --> 03:32.612
I have to be on the part of the solution,
not a part of the problem.

03:34.080 --> 03:35.949
The fact that I grew up poor

03:35.949 --> 03:39.419
in a rural community, and I'm here

03:39.619 --> 03:44.390
being able to lift up voices of people
from my community and find solutions.

03:44.591 --> 03:48.228
I think if I can do it, and faced
with the type of inequality

03:48.228 --> 03:52.232
as an African-American female
in this country, anyone can do it.
